//github.com/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commit/22ca5e1a85d25d20d8231f81a56e6a65ca36cbf6","sha":"22ca5e1a85d25d20d8231f81a56e6a65ca36cbf6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/22ca5e1a85d25d20d8231f81a56e6a65ca36cbf6"}],"sha":"f5aee5ac8e5cc33d819f71158aa7046ddebea98e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/f5aee5ac8e5cc33d819f71158aa7046ddebea98e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/220909633?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan","id":220909633,"login":"analisaperlengkapan","node_id":"U_kgDODSrQQQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/22ca5e1a85d25d20d8231f81a56e6a65ca36cbf6/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-29T00:07:34Z","email":"analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com","name":"analisaperlengkapan"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-29T00:07:34Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(perlengkapan): the report asked for a satker code and hid what it counted (#909)\n\n* feat(perlengkapan): the report asked for a satker code and hid what it counted\n\nTwo things on the pakaian dinas report page, both invisible from the outside.\n\n**The Satker filter was a free-text box** asking for a `kode_satker` — a code\nnobody carries in their head, where one wrong digit returns an empty report\nthat looks exactly like a satker with nothing to report. It is now a dropdown\nfed by the campaign''s own participant list, which is already scoped\nserver-side, so it cannot offer a satker whose report the caller may not read.\nIt re-fetches when the period changes and empties when the period is cleared:\na satker left over from another campaign would silently narrow the next report\nto nothing.\n\n**Every laporan query counts only satker that reached `Selesai`.** That is\ndeliberate — a recap is a procurement figure, and sizes a wilayah validator has\nnot accepted should not be ordered against. But it was four bare `1008`\nliterals in four query strings with nothing naming them, and its effect could\nnot be seen: mid-campaign the report simply comes back small, and a recap\ncovering 3 of 238 satker renders identically to a complete one.\n\nThe page now states its coverage. No new endpoint and no DTO change were\nneeded — `satker_selesai` and `total_satker` are already on the campaign this\npage fetches for its own dropdown, computed with the same predicate. They were\nin hand all along and never shown.\n\nThree states, not two: a campaign with no participants at all is not \"0 of 0\nfinished, so the numbers are not final\". Staging carries exactly such a\ncampaign, which is how that was caught.\n\n**Naming the constant nearly shipped a runtime bug.** The two raw blocks were\n`r#\"…\"#.to_string()`, where `{AKTIVITAS_SELESAI}` is literal text, not a value.\nPostgres rejects that; `cargo check` does not notice. The integration test is\nwhat proves the query still parses, and its canary confirms it: reverting one\nblock to a non-interpolating string returns `400 db error`.\n\n**And the screenshot found a third thing.** The coverage line rendered with a\ncorrect amber background and slate text. `success/warning/danger/info` were\ndefined as `50 100 400 500 600 700 900` — no `200`, no `300` — while the tree\nreferences those shades **115 times**. Tailwind emits nothing for a shade it\ndoes not know: no warning, no build error, the class stays in the HTML and the\nelement inherits whatever colour was around it. Every status message in\nperlengkapan has been rendering in plain slate on a correctly-coloured panel.\n\nMeasured before and after on the same build: `text-warning-300` went from\n`rgb(241,245,249)` to `rgb(252,211,77)`, `text-success-300` to\n`rgb(110,231,183)`. Same class of bug as `bg-primary` in #903.\n\n`check-tailwind-shades-exist.py` derives the used set from each config''s OWN\ncontent globs — so `lib/ui` is checked against every config that pulls it in —\nand the defined set from that same config. Canaries: removing 300 again fails\nwith 5 findings; a glob matching no file exits 2.\n\nTests assert the filter''s effect, not its text: a satker at `SubmitToPusat` is\nabsent from the recap and one at `Selesai` is present. Removing the predicate\nmakes both tests fail with the count at 2 instead of 1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\n* fix(perlengkapan): the report''s own test still typed into a dropdown\n\nThe satker filter became a `<select>` fed by the campaign''s participants.\n`pakaian-workflow.spec.ts` still drove it with `.fill()`, which a `<select>`\nrejects outright — so the e2e failed on the change that was the point of the\nPR. The element type is part of the contract here, and a test that types into\nthe control is asserting the old one.\n\nThree things follow from that, all in this commit:\n\n* the filter is driven with `selectOption`, and `blur()` goes away with the\n  text box that needed it;\n* a new test covers what nothing covered: that the dropdown lists the\n  campaign''s OWN satker (count + names, order-independent, so a list leaking\n  another campaign''s satker fails) and that the coverage line states how much\n  of the campaign the numbers cover. Both directions are asserted — seed 6b is\n  complete and 6a never is, in either test order;\n* `AKTIVITAS_SELESAI` reaches the two queries that actually compute\n  `satker_selesai`/`total_satker` for that line. They live in `pengajuan.rs`\n  and were still bare `1008`s, which makes the constant''s own doc comment\n  wrong: six sites, not four. Counting them by hand was the mistake; a census\n  found the two I missed.\n\nOne of those two is a plain `r#\"...\"#` handed straight to `query_opt`, where\n`{AKTIVITAS_SELESAI}` would have been literal text that compiles clean and\nfails in Postgres at request time — the same trap as in `laporan.rs`, so it\nbecame a `format!` too.\n\nAlso un-wraps the coverage sentence: it had been joined into one literal with\nits indentation inside the string. HTML collapses that, which is exactly why\nit was invisible on screen while every other reader kept the gap.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b14d6c593a06a48908813e125a841b8b5eea4622","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/trees/b14d6c593a06a48908813e125a841b8b5eea4622"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/commits/22ca5e1a85d25d20d8231f81a56e6a65ca36cbf6","verification":{"payload":"tree b14d6c593a06a48908813e125a841b8b5eea4622\nparent 30482a2242aa95b3c11a1fcb0d8c204a0ec61e64\nauthor analisaperlengkapan <analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com> 1790640454 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790640454 +0000\n\nfeat(perlengkapan): the report asked for a satker code and hid what it counted (#909)\n\n* feat(perlengkapan): the report asked for a satker code and hid what it counted\n\nTwo things on the pakaian dinas report page, both invisible from the outside.\n\n**The Satker filter was a free-text box** asking for a `kode_satker` — a code\nnobody carries in their head, where one wrong digit returns an empty report\nthat looks exactly like a satker with nothing to report. It is now a dropdown\nfed by the campaign''s own participant list, which is already scoped\nserver-side, so it cannot offer a satker whose report the caller may not read.\nIt re-fetches when the period changes and empties when the period is cleared:\na satker left over from another campaign would silently narrow the next report\nto nothing.\n\n**Every laporan query counts only satker that reached `Selesai`.** That is\ndeliberate — a recap is a procurement figure, and sizes a wilayah validator has\nnot accepted should not be ordered against. But it was four bare `1008`\nliterals in four query strings with nothing naming them, and its effect could\nnot be seen: mid-campaign the report simply comes back small, and a recap\ncovering 3 of 238 satker renders identically to a complete one.\n\nThe page now states its coverage. No new endpoint and no DTO change were\nneeded — `satker_selesai` and `total_satker` are already on the campaign this\npage fetches for its own dropdown, computed with the same predicate. They were\nin hand all along and never shown.\n\nThree states, not two: a campaign with no participants at all is not \"0 of 0\nfinished, so the numbers are not final\". Staging carries exactly such a\ncampaign, which is how that was caught.\n\n**Naming the constant nearly shipped a runtime bug.** The two raw blocks were\n`r#\"…\"#.to_string()`, where `{AKTIVITAS_SELESAI}` is literal text, not a value.\nPostgres rejects that; `cargo check` does not notice. The integration test is\nwhat proves the query still parses, and its canary confirms it: reverting one\nblock to a non-interpolating string returns `400 db error`.\n\n**And the screenshot found a third thing.** The coverage line rendered with a\ncorrect amber background and slate text. `success/warning/danger/info` were\ndefined as `50 100 400 500 600 700 900` — no `200`, no `300` — while the tree\nreferences those shades **115 times**. Tailwind emits nothing for a shade it\ndoes not know: no warning, no build error, the class stays in the HTML and the\nelement inherits whatever colour was around it. Every status message in\nperlengkapan has been rendering in plain slate on a correctly-coloured panel.\n\nMeasured before and after on the same build: `text-warning-300` went from\n`rgb(241,245,249)` to `rgb(252,211,77)`, `text-success-300` to\n`rgb(110,231,183)`. Same class of bug as `bg-primary` in #903.\n\n`check-tailwind-shades-exist.py` derives the used set from each config''s OWN\ncontent globs — so `lib/ui` is checked against every config that pulls it in —\nand the defined set from that same config. Canaries: removing 300 again fails\nwith 5 findings; a glob matching no file exits 2.\n\nTests assert the filter''s effect, not its text: a satker at `SubmitToPusat` is\nabsent from the recap and one at `Selesai` is present. Removing the predicate\nmakes both tests fail with the count at 2 instead of 1.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\n* fix(perlengkapan): the report''s own test still typed into a dropdown\n\nThe satker filter became a `<select>` fed by the campaign''s participants.\n`pakaian-workflow.spec.ts` still drove it with `.fill()`, which a `<select>`\nrejects outright — so the e2e failed on the change that was the point of the\nPR. The element type is part of the contract here, and a test that types into\nthe control is asserting the old one.\n\nThree things follow from that, all in this commit:\n\n* the filter is driven with `selectOption`, and `blur()` goes away with the\n  text box that needed it;\n* a new test covers what nothing covered: that the dropdown lists the\n  campaign''s OWN satker (count + names, order-independent, so a list leaking\n  another campaign''s satker fails) and that the coverage line states how much\n  of the campaign the numbers cover. Both directions are asserted — seed 6b is\n  complete and 6a never is, in either test order;\n* `AKTIVITAS_SELESAI` reaches the two queries that actually compute\n  `satker_selesai`/`total_satker` for that line. They live in `pengajuan.rs`\n  and were still bare `1008`s, which makes the constant''s own doc comment\n  wrong: six sites, not four. Counting them by hand was the mistake; a census\n  found the two I missed.\n\nOne of those two is a plain `r#\"...\"#` handed straight to `query_opt`, where\n`{AKTIVITAS_SELESAI}` would have been literal text that compiles clean and\nfails in Postgres at request time — the same trap as in `laporan.rs`, so it\nbecame a `format!` too.\n\nAlso un-wraps the coverage sentence: it had been joined into one literal with\nits indentation inside the string. HTML collapses that, which is exactly why\nit was invisible on screen while every other reader kept the gap.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJquwFGCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAJbgQAItDXzjq7sTnTnRVoo+62miZ\ntKzYCaQD4+iz6DZ8E6me+JNJgCFwuKTMpvcuqKrUQ4UDSAeq8MT73cmU/ZgV/ncs\nYK4uCroAFIVKxaqT3mXMVB/5n4zK115xUlxjnJeIfCEX04vTmtsAnbLdK8uCa23i\nu7YjfALEmUPiW41a0pB8H8/sXg5x/1uABo3WkevjrLLTiiBZ94BJtswz028asyh2\nxcLy+xI9BKPTPwL7YQFRTjnkpw00kG3VaSLwRM5/zWFR0rfNtoSaI7OJyt8asmh6\nSdNfAlgex8kbGYDUKZo91M190+leKRE9x3EbELgpcHI8mIYfgQgSc4R3gsF8JNEq\nO9KACpZuedLHSjL3skCxhCnUjgebaSn+owZt1+FXukDT+tFMJJpqxwmqaR7jKx15\nTajXpFzpciJETU1mS8xNkZaSYnGQ4BzFUhEJVJKwQcFhlb2CxZK1/AiHokAauEWD\nmwHsZww6D4eWXfPR7xP58wrw33ssGd7dNq/fPB9Og+U+bnTHwW8cttAaEDB12lxi