//api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/74bc6be1e88c1e4abbd5da547eb73228649d87f8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-28T05:54:52Z","email":"analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com","name":"analisaperlengkapan"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-28T05:54:52Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"deps: rustls 0.23.43 -> 0.23.45 (RUSTSEC-2026-0285) (#928)\n\nThe Security workflow has been failing on `main` since at least 2026-09-16,\nand not on an unmaintained-crate notice: RUSTSEC-2026-0285 is a real rustls\nflaw. TLS 1.3 handshake messages packed after a key-changing message in the\nsame record were accepted at the wrong encryption level — RFC 8446 §5.1 says\nto terminate the connection with `unexpected_message` instead. The transcript\nstays authenticated, so a network attacker cannot alter or complete a\nhandshake; the effect is that a peer can send in plaintext what should have\nbeen encrypted and rustls does not object. Same bug as Go''s CVE-2025-61730.\n\nPatched in 0.23.45, a patch-level bump inside the range the manifests already\nallow, so this is `cargo update -p rustls` and nothing else. `rustls-webpki`\n0.103.13 -> 0.103.15 comes along as its dependency.\n\nVerified both directions, because \"the check is green now\" does not by itself\nsay this change is what made it green:\n\n  old Cargo.lock -> `advisories FAILED`, the finding naming RUSTSEC-2026-0285\n  new Cargo.lock -> `advisories ok`\n\nand `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` is clean on the new lock (only the\npre-existing dual-cfg dead-code warnings in the perlengkapan frontend, which\nare unrelated and already on record).\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"14f06489a2a21ad7ba0905d614748a20b4ccaa55","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/trees/14f06489a2a21ad7ba0905d614748a20b4ccaa55"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/commits/74bc6be1e88c1e4abbd5da547eb73228649d87f8","verification":{"payload":"tree 14f06489a2a21ad7ba0905d614748a20b4ccaa55\nparent b2626f3f558513ab45dc2f88d3f8bf9e25dfb37a\nauthor analisaperlengkapan <analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com> 1790574892 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790574892 +0000\n\ndeps: rustls 0.23.43 -> 0.23.45 (RUSTSEC-2026-0285) (#928)\n\nThe Security workflow has been failing on `main` since at least 2026-09-16,\nand not on an unmaintained-crate notice: RUSTSEC-2026-0285 is a real rustls\nflaw. TLS 1.3 handshake messages packed after a key-changing message in the\nsame record were accepted at the wrong encryption level — RFC 8446 §5.1 says\nto terminate the connection with `unexpected_message` instead. The transcript\nstays authenticated, so a network attacker cannot alter or complete a\nhandshake; the effect is that a peer can send in plaintext what should have\nbeen encrypted and rustls does not object. Same bug as Go''s CVE-2025-61730.\n\nPatched in 0.23.45, a patch-level bump inside the range the manifests already\nallow, so this is `cargo update -p rustls` and nothing else. `rustls-webpki`\n0.103.13 -> 0.103.15 comes along as its dependency.\n\nVerified both directions, because \"the check is green now\" does not by itself\nsay this change is what made it green:\n\n  old Cargo.lock -> `advisories FAILED`, the finding naming RUSTSEC-2026-0285\n  new Cargo.lock -> `advisories ok`\n\nand `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` is clean on the new lock (only the\npre-existing dual-cfg dead-code warnings in the perlengkapan frontend, which\nare unrelated and already on record).\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqugEsCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAMXsQABwlMHnXPIdns7sc7yvJ8QQI\nMVy8oDMIdVXraitGe4dsJAy/lUg2VEsF7A83+fuqmyXAeebzn