an/simpel2/commits/33a0fd57f97dc8fbcb74f6a68d5fe743c495e1e8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/220909633?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan","id":220909633,"login":"analisaperlengkapan","node_id":"U_kgDODSrQQQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/38b0fec94d6ae921cb6d699b775223891fbcdbeb/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-02T01:08:26Z","email":"analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com","name":"analisaperlengkapan"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-02T01:08:26Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(perlengkapan): the pakaian dinas workflow had an approval tail and no head (#902)\n\n* feat(perlengkapan): the pakaian dinas workflow had an approval tail and no head\n\nNothing in the backend ever wrote `pengajuan_pakaian_dinas_satker_pegawai` or\n`..._pegawai_ukuran`. Both tables appear in exactly two places: reads (the\nreport, the dashboard) and the cascade DELETE when a campaign is removed. The\nrows staging holds came from the e2e seed, not from the application. So a\nsatker had no way to record who needs a uniform or what size, and the approval\nchain behind it — wilayah to pusat, which does work — had nothing to approve.\n\nOperators also had no way to submit: `available_actions` in the satker\ncomponent only knew the validator stages 1001 and 1004, and never the operator''s\nown 1000/1003 -> submit.\n\nThis adds the missing half. Open the campaign, see your own satker''s roster,\npress \"Isi Ukuran\" on a row, fill the sizes in a small dialog, save, and land\nback on the list already updated. Then \"Ajukan\" once, at the end.\n\nTwo decisions worth stating.\n\nThe columns belong to the CAMPAIGN, not to the code. A pengajuan chooses which\nspesifikasi it asks for, and that choice is frozen into\n`pengajuan_pakaian_dinas_pakaian`, so a PDH campaign shows Pakaian Dinas /\nCelana / Sepatu Dinas and another campaign shows its own. Three hard-coded\nbaju/celana/sepatu columns would be wrong on the first campaign shaped\ndifferently — which is also why the report''s existing\n`MAX(CASE WHEN ukuran_group = ...)` can only ever answer for those three.\nGendered rows are offered only to the people they apply to, and a column that\ndoes not apply reads \"—\" rather than sharing the blank that means \"not yet\nfilled\".\n\nSaving is per employee, not one giant submit at the end. simpelv1 keeps the\nwhole roster in hidden inputs behind a single Simpan; a satker with hundreds of\npeople then loses everything to one failure on the last step. The identity is\nnever sent by the client either: it is read from `integrasi.mysimkari_pegawai`\nat save time and frozen onto the row, because jabatan and pangkat change with\nmutasi and promosi and the submission has to record what was true when it was\nmade — and because letting the client send it means letting someone else''s name\nbe rewritten from outside.\n\nScoping follows the module''s existing rule: out of scope answers NotFound, not\nForbidden, so another satker''s roster cannot be confirmed to exist from a status\ncode. Writes are refused by the server once the satker has left 1000/1003, not\nmerely by a disabled button, and a `pakaian_id` from a different campaign is\nrejected rather than stored where a report would later find it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WQ99i4hsHtgtPr4pHRk6Vn\n\n* fix(perlengkapan): the satker''s own submit named a role that does not exist\n\nRunning the new pengisian tests turned up two things neither `cargo check` nor\nclippy could see, and one of them would have stopped the workflow at its last\nstep even with everything else fixed.\n\n**The submit transition was unreachable.** `determine_next_status` matched\n`(Input, \"submit\", \"pelaksana\")`. The IAM seeds operator_satker,\nvalidator_satker, approver_satker, validator_wilayah, validator_pusat and\nadmin; `pelaksana` is none of them, and neither frontend has ever mentioned it.\nSo every \"Ajukan\" a satker could make was refused with \"Aksi ''submit'' tidak\nvalid untuk status ''Input''\". It now names `operator_satker`, the role that\nactually fills the form, with `admin` accepted as at the validator stages.\n\nIt survived because the test invented the role: `action(\"pelaksana\", \"submit\")`\nput a string in a header that no IAM would ever issue, certifying a step no\nreal user could take. That test now uses a real role.\n\n**The participation-row INSERT was rejected outright.** Written as\n`SELECT $1, $2, 1000, $3`, Postgres deduced `$2` as `text` from the select list\nand `character varying` from the NOT EXISTS comparison, and refused the whole\nstatement: \"inconsistent types deduced for parameter $2\". Reproduced in a clean\ncontainer, fixed with explicit casts, and re-verified — including that running\nit twice still leaves one row. Without this, creating a campaign would have\nfailed entirely in production, not merely lost its satker rows.\n\n`pakaian_dinas_campaign_scope_test` also seeded its own participation rows,\nunder a comment saying no API path made them. That premise is gone, so with\nboth writers there were two rows per satker — two statuses for one thing. The\ntest now LOOKS UP the row the production path creates, which strengthens it:\nif campaign creation stops making them, `query_one` fails loudly instead of the\ntest quietly papering over it with a row of its own.\n\n28 of 28 pakaian dinas integration tests pass against a real Postgres.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WQ99i4hsHtgtPr4pHRk6Vn\n\n* test(perlengkapan): the roster tests were sending \"Bearer [object Object]\"\n\nThe four new roster tests read `apiLogin`''s result — which resolves to\n`{ accessToken, refreshToken }` — straight into a template string, so every\nrequest carried the literal `Bearer [object Object]` and authenc answered 401.\nThe cross-satker test made that visible (`expected 404, received 401`); the\nother three read `.data` off the unchecked response and surfaced as\n`Cannot read properties of undefined`, which says nothing about the cause.\n\nThey now use the shared `tokenFor`, which returns a string, and go through a\n`fetchObject` reader that asserts the status and prints the body first — the\nobject-shaped sibling of the `fetchRows` helper added with the spesifikasi\nfix. Neither reader lets a call site touch `.data` before knowing the request\nsucceeded.\n\nRebased onto the spesifikasi branch, whose own fix this shares.\n29/29 pakaian-dinas integration tests pass against real Postgres.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WQ99i4hsHtgtPr4pHRk6Vn\n\n* fix(e2e): the fill-in campaign had no clothing types, so there was nothing to fill\n\nThe roster test failed on `pengisian-tabel` not being visible, three attempts\nidentically. The page was right and the fixture was wrong: the size table only\nrenders when the campaign carries at least one clothing type, and\n`pengajuan_pakaian_dinas_pakaian` was seeded ONLY for the reporting campaign\n(…c2), never for the fill-in one (…c1) the test navigates to. The fixture\ndescribed a campaign that could be reported on but never filled — which is not\na state the application can produce.\n\nThe failure message pointed at the wrong thing, and that is the second half of\nthis change. `element(s) not found` covers three different causes: the route\ndid not resolve, the campaign has no clothing types so the page declines to\ndraw a table, or the table is genuinely broken. Only the last is the test''s\nsubject. The test now polls for a NAMED state and asserts it is \"tabel\", so the\nnext failure says which of the three it is.\n\nTo make that possible, two states got structural identities: the 404 page\n(`halaman-404`) and the empty-campaign notice\n(`pengisian-tanpa-jenis-pakaian`). The 404 testid is the one worth keeping\nbeyond this test — a route sweep with no way to recognise a 404 reports it as\n\"a nearly empty page\", which it has done before.\n\nBoth are `data-testid`, not text. `getByText` is a case-insensitive SUBSTRING\nmatch, so an assertion written against the sentence can pass while reading a\ndifferent element entirely.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"63004a34fcce84a14e94241f72254bc9f1a3952a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/trees/63004a34fcce84a14e94241f72254bc9f1a3952a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/commits/38b0fec94d6ae921cb6d699b775223891fbcdbeb","verification":{"payload":"tree 63004a34fcce84a14e94241f72254bc9f1a3952a\nparent 46daa4ffe41b7f717b416e0c89177ace9e8ac314\nauthor analisaperlengkapan <analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com> 1788311306 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788311306 +0700\n\nfeat(perlengkapan): the pakaian dinas workflow had an approval tail and no head (#902)\n\n* feat(perlengkapan): the pakaian dinas workflow had an approval tail and no head\n\nNothing in the backend ever wrote `pengajuan_pakaian_dinas_satker_pegawai` or\n`..._pegawai_ukuran`. Both tables appear in exactly two places: reads (the\nreport, the dashboard) and the cascade DELETE when a campaign is removed. The\nrows staging holds came from the e2e seed, not from the application. So a\nsatker had no way to record who needs a uniform or what size, and the approval\nchain behind it — wilayah to pusat, which does work — had nothing to approve.\n\nOperators also had no way to submit: `available_actions` in the satker\ncomponent only knew the validator stages 1001 and 1004, and never the operator''s\nown 1000/1003 -> submit.\n\nThis adds the missing half. Open the campaign, see your own satker''s roster,\npress \"Isi Ukuran\" on a row, fill the sizes in a small dialog, save, and land\nback on the list already updated. Then \"Ajukan\" once, at the end.\n\nTwo decisions worth stating.\n\nThe columns belong to the CAMPAIGN, not to the code. A pengajuan chooses which\nspesifikasi it asks for, and that choice is frozen into\n`pengajuan_pakaian_dinas_pakaian`, so a PDH campaign shows Pakaian Dinas /\nCelana / Sepatu Dinas and another campaign shows its own. Three hard-coded\nbaju/celana/sepatu columns would be wrong on the first campaign shaped\ndifferently — which is also why the report''s existing\n`MAX(CASE WHEN ukuran_group = ...)` can only ever answer for those three.\nGendered rows are offered only to the people they apply to, and a column that\ndoes not apply reads \"—\" rather than sharing the blank that means \"not yet\nfilled\".\n\nSaving is per employee, not one giant submit at the end. simpelv1 keeps the\nwhole roster in hidden inputs behind a single Simpan; a satker with hundreds of\npeople then loses everything to one failure on the last step. The identity is\nnever sent by the client either: it is read from `integrasi.mysimkari_pegawai`\nat save time and frozen onto the row, because jabatan and pangkat change with\nmutasi and promosi and the submission has to record what was true when it was\nmade — and because letting the client send it means letting someone else''s name\nbe rewritten from outside.\n\nScoping follows the module''s existing rule: out of scope answers NotFound, not\nForbidden, so another satker''s roster cannot be confirmed to exist from a status\ncode. Writes are refused by the server once the satker has left 1000/1003, not\nmerely by a disabled button, and a `pakaian_id` from a different campaign is\nrejected rather than stored where a report would later find it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WQ99i4hsHtgtPr4pHRk6Vn\n\n* fix(perlengkapan): the satker''s own submit named a role that does not exist\n\nRunning the new pengisian tests turned up two things neither `cargo check` nor\nclippy could see, and one of them would have stopped the workflow at its last\nstep even with everything else fixed.\n\n**The submit transition was unreachable.** `determine_next_status` matched\n`(Input, \"submit\", \"pelaksana\")`. The IAM seeds operator_satker,\nvalidator_satker, approver_satker, validator_wilayah, validator_pusat and\nadmin; `pelaksana` is none of them, and neither frontend has ever mentioned it.\nSo every \"Ajukan\" a satker could make was refused with \"Aksi ''submit'' tidak\nvalid untuk status ''Input''\". It now names `operator_satker`, the role that\nactually fills the form, with `admin` accepted as at the validator stages.\n\nIt survived because the test invented the role: `action(\"pelaksana\", \"submit\")`\nput a string in a header that no IAM would ever issue, certifying a step no\nreal user could take. That test now uses a real role.\n\n**The participation-row INSERT was rejected outright.** Written as\n`SELECT $1, $2, 1000, $3`, Postgres deduced `$2` as `text` from the select list\nand `character varying` from the NOT EXISTS comparison, and refused the whole\nstatement: \"inconsistent types deduced for parameter $2\". Reproduced in a clean\ncontainer, fixed with explicit casts, and re-verified — including that running\nit twice still leaves one row. Without this, creating a campaign would have\nfailed entirely in production, not merely lost its satker rows.\n\n`pakaian_dinas_campaign_scope_test` also seeded its own participation rows,\nunder a comment saying no API path made them. That premise is gone, so with\nboth writers there were two rows per satker — two statuses for one thing. The\ntest now LOOKS UP the row the production path creates, which strengthens it:\nif campaign creation stops making them, `query_one` fails loudly instead of the\ntest quietly papering over it with a row of its own.\n\n28 of 28 pakaian dinas integration tests pass against a real Postgres.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WQ99i4hsHtgtPr4pHRk6Vn\n\n* test(perlengkapan): the roster tests were sending \"Bearer [object Object]\"\n\nThe four new roster tests read `apiLogin`''s result — which resolves to\n`{ accessToken, refreshToken }` — straight into a template string, so every\nrequest carried the literal `Bearer [object Object]` and authenc answered 401.\nThe cross-satker test made that visible (`expected 404, received 401`); the\nother three read `.data` off the unchecked response and surfaced as\n`Cannot read properties of undefined`, which says nothing about the cause.\n\nThey now use the shared `tokenFor`, which returns a string, and go through a\n`fetchObject` reader that asserts the status and prints the body first — the\nobject-shaped sibling of the `fetchRows` helper added with the spesifikasi\nfix. Neither reader lets a call site touch `.data` before knowing the request\nsucceeded.\n\nRebased onto the spesifikasi branch, whose own fix this shares.\n29/29 pakaian-dinas integration tests pass against real Postgres.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WQ99i4hsHtgtPr4pHRk6Vn\n\n* fix(e2e): the fill-in campaign had no clothing types, so there was nothing to fill\n\nThe roster test failed on `pengisian-tabel` not being visible, three attempts\nidentically. The page was right and the fixture was wrong: the size table only\nrenders when the campaign carries at least one clothing type, and\n`pengajuan_pakaian_dinas_pakaian` was seeded ONLY for the reporting campaign\n(…c2), never for the fill-in one (…c1) the test navigates to. The fixture\ndescribed a campaign that could be reported on but never filled — which is not\na state the application can produce.\n\nThe failure message pointed at the wrong thing, and that is the second half of\nthis change. `element(s) not found` covers three different causes: the route\ndid not resolve, the campaign has no clothing types so the page declines to\ndraw a table, or the table is genuinely broken. Only the last is the test''s\nsubject. The test now polls for a NAMED state and asserts it is \"tabel\", so the\nnext failure says which of the three it is.\n\nTo make that possible, two states got structural identities: the 404 page\n(`halaman-404`) and the empty-campaign notice\n(`pengisian-tanpa-jenis-pakaian`). The 404 testid is the one worth keeping\nbeyond this test — a route sweep with no way to recognise a 404 reports it as\n\"a nearly empty page\", which it has done before.\n\nBoth are `data-testid`, not text. `getByText` is a case-insensitive SUBSTRING\nmatch, so an assertion written against the sentence can pass while reading a\ndifferent element entirely.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJql3cKCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAGKIQAGvitx3RJjpxjk9C8SKkpFPX\n0DOeWhSNO2nOkWaDTGm/cGb4dKpAK25wMtl9PBGQyva2u2m1jqbXcRG4EsQcQu2i\nEqqWp/+puj4TwdPjBo5uIdwVPkxG4NduhJID+YrzB60Ba1XSC02hcGzzXhIWTbid\n2RJk2NAD15TP8yWHLheB9LUQ0Q7TwnFCfr4LZ3NeN5w/4BMDakZGTt4nth/cLEU0\nJHJ6BqUGCuewuejDUFLBaCP7B5piQIWI4/kbaHnH7dAE1HP5oOLDvLjcQ64+UXtT\nUZnyvqu9th2JVQ6vyyHNhHJ1V5l49GPqqlmlagwKOezjHCKbPg2O494SLn6E7W1W\nqevWI8d+RQUagLROwgaSYcRMqaG2zVQT/+CRD3U25d0HUvg52mbhpqoFP1RZHFZ4\nIp369jlkh9WTASld36SQ7WXVjhzZgiMT5mR2pzWmmU6rQfFGXqpwyEG3/0HYPD3N\nu3xfp238S1bpgn75v7xhPoNZz2j8BPY94OXRoBm4VJ+2xeaMVsDtIcUQDhWdI62n\nidRD8S87VOkhP1w3ad8XZ8qtppaIEUR53nRApTqshtD5CKobzdxLK3Bqp1qxFGAP\no3XDGRQCj4cUURAnXP6pfE/UE1ZSvjFsqyNUHCABhqC+M8mPZz1zng4o8sljVT+g\nkgOeDup2OE5kYIcynxNB\n=MMp7\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-02T01:08:27Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commit/38b0fec94d6ae921cb6d699b775223891fbcdbeb","node_id":"C_kwDOP93DxNoAKDM4YjBmZWM5NGQ2YWU5MjFjYjZkNjk5Yjc3NTIyMzg5MWZiY2RiZWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commit/46daa4ffe41b7f717b416e0c89177ace9e8ac314","sha":"46daa4ffe41b7f717b416e0c89177ace9e8ac314","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/46daa4ffe41b7f717b416e0c89177ace9e8ac314"}],"sha":"38b0fec94d6ae921cb6d699b775223891fbcdbeb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/38b0fec94d6ae921cb6d699b775223891fbcdbeb"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/220909633?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan","id":220909633,"login":"analisaperlengkapan","node_id":"U_kgDODSrQQQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/46daa4ffe41b7f717b416e0c89177ace9e8ac314/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-02T01:08:06Z","email":"analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com","name":"analisaperlengkapan"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-02T01:08:06Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(portal): the photo reached the database and stopped one layer short (#904)\n\n* feat(portal): the photo reached the database and stopped one layer short\n\nThe column has been there since `001_init_schema.sql`. Nothing read it out.\n`message MysimkariPegawai` stops at field 11, the `SELECT` in\n`get_mysimkari_pegawai` never names it, and `/auth/me` returns a hard-coded\n`avatar: None`. So the photo sat in the database and reached no screen.\n\nThree links had to be added to carry it: `foto = 12` on the proto,\n`COALESCE(p.foto, '''') AS foto` on the query, and a resolver on `/auth/me`.\n\nIt is called `foto`, not `avatar`. `avatar` is an IAM concept — an image the\nuser uploads, and nobody ever has. This is employment data owned by integrasi,\nand `foto` is what `PegawaiInfo` in perlengkapan already calls it. One name for\none thing across both frontends.\n\nIt is resolved, not stored. authenc is pure IAM; a `foto` column there would be\na second copy of integrasi''s data. `resolve_satuan_kerja` already does exactly\nthis, so the photo rides the proven path with the same cache TTL, and a failed\nresolve means \"no photo\" and falls back to initials — it never blocks login.\n\nTwo things this uncovered.\n\nauthenc in `docker-compose.yml` never had `INTEGRASI_GRPC_URL`. The cluster\nalways gave it one; compose never did. So `state.integrasi_client` was `None`\nin every e2e run and the entire fetch-at-read path was exercised nowhere —\nincluding `satuan_kerja`, which had been quietly returning the raw satker code\nthis whole time. `/auth/me` answers 200 either way, which is why nobody\nnoticed. One of the new tests now asserts `satuan_kerja` is a name, not a code;\nthat assertion alone would have caught it.\n\n`integrasi.proto` has two copies. `layanan/authenc/proto/` keeps its own (also\n`secreton.proto` and `common.proto`) because authenc''s Dockerfile copies only\n`layanan/authenc` into the build context. Adding a field to the owner alone is\na silent one-way break: prost ignores unknown fields, so a consumer built from\nthe stale copy never receives the value and nothing fails to compile. Here I\nwas lucky — `foto` is used directly, so the compiler complained.\n\n`check-proto-copies-agree.py` compares every `.proto` that exists more than\nonce, after comments and blank lines are stripped. Canaries: removing `foto`\nfrom the authenc copy exits 1 naming the differing lines; a scan that finds no\nduplicate at all exits 2, so a broken derivation cannot read as clean. All\nthree pairs agree exactly today.\n\nScreenshots before and after: the profile page had no photo anywhere and the\nnavbar drew a generic person SVG. With the media host stubbed the run also\nproved the browser builds the URL correctly, percent-encoding the seeded name''s\nspaces and parentheses against the real host.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\n* fix(portal): the host-target mock never got the field the wasm build required\n\n`UserInfo` gained `foto`, and `authenc_api.rs` builds it in two places: the\nreal wasm client, and a `#[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]` mock used by the\nhost-target build. I verified with `cargo clippy --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`,\nwhich compiles exactly one of those two arms — so the second construction site\nnever entered a build I ran, and CI''s host-target `Test Rust (antarmuka-portal)`\nfound it as E0063.\n\nThis crate is dual-cfg by design; checking one target is checking half of it.\n\nThe mock returns a file NAME, matching the shape the real `/auth/me` sends —\nnot a URL and not an empty string, either of which would let a consumer that\nmishandles one of them pass against this stub.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"6f6775ba04560d17ea085669be10adcd4e13f33a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/trees/6f6775ba04560d17ea085669be10adcd4e13f33a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/commits/46daa4ffe41b7f717b416e0c89177ace9e8ac314","verification":{"payload":"tree 6f6775ba04560d17ea085669be10adcd4e13f33a\nparent 23b19eee96cc17f5aaf5cb2d5f5713f627f6b7bd\nauthor analisaperlengkapan <analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com> 1788311286 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788311286 +0700\n\nfeat(portal): the photo reached the database and stopped one layer short (#904)\n\n* feat(portal): the photo reached the database and stopped one layer short\n\nThe column has been there since `001_init_schema.sql`. Nothing read it out.\n`message MysimkariPegawai` stops at field 11, the `SELECT` in\n`get_mysimkari_pegawai` never names it, and `/auth/me` returns a hard-coded\n`avatar: None`. So the photo sat in the database and reached no screen.\n\nThree links had to be added to carry it: `foto = 12` on the proto,\n`COALESCE(p.foto, '''') AS foto` on the query, and a resolver on `/auth/me`.\n\nIt is called `foto`, not `avatar`. `avatar` is an IAM concept — an image the\nuser uploads, and nobody ever has. This is employment data owned by integrasi,\nand `foto` is what `PegawaiInfo` in perlengkapan already calls it. One name for\none thing across both frontends.\n\nIt is resolved, not stored. authenc is pure IAM; a `foto` column there would be\na second copy of integrasi''s data. `resolve_satuan_kerja` already does exactly\nthis, so the photo rides the proven path with the same cache TTL, and a failed\nresolve means \"no photo\" and falls back to initials — it never blocks login.\n\nTwo things this uncovered.\n\nauthenc in `docker-compose.yml` never had `INTEGRASI_GRPC_URL`. The cluster\nalways gave it one; compose never did. So `state.integrasi_client` was `None`\nin every e2e run and the entire fetch-at-read path was exercised nowhere —\nincluding `satuan_kerja`, which had been quietly returning the raw satker code\nthis whole time. `/auth/me` answers 200 either way, which is why nobody\nnoticed. One of the new tests now asserts `satuan_kerja` is a name, not a code;\nthat assertion alone would have caught it.\n\n`integrasi.proto` has two copies. `layanan/authenc/proto/` keeps its own (also\n`secreton.proto` and `common.proto`) because authenc''s Dockerfile copies only\n`layanan/authenc` into the build context. Adding a field to the owner alone is\na silent one-way break: prost ignores unknown fields, so a consumer built from\nthe stale copy never receives the value and nothing fails to compile. Here I\nwas lucky — `foto` is used directly, so the compiler complained.\n\n`check-proto-copies-agree.py` compares every `.proto` that exists more than\nonce, after comments and blank lines are stripped. Canaries: removing `foto`\nfrom the authenc copy exits 1 naming the differing lines; a scan that finds no\nduplicate at all exits 2, so a broken derivation cannot read as clean. All\nthree pairs agree exactly today.\n\nScreenshots before and after: the profile page had no photo anywhere and the\nnavbar drew a generic person SVG. With the media host stubbed the run also\nproved the browser builds the URL correctly, percent-encoding the seeded name''s\nspaces and parentheses against the real host.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\n* fix(portal): the host-target mock never got the field the wasm build required\n\n`UserInfo` gained `foto`, and `authenc_api.rs` builds it in two places: the\nreal wasm client, and a `#[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]` mock used by the\nhost-target build. I verified with `cargo clippy --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`,\nwhich compiles exactly one of those two arms — so the second construction site\nnever entered a build I ran, and CI''s host-target `Test Rust (antarmuka-portal)`\nfound it as E0063.\n\nThis crate is dual-cfg by design; checking one target is checking half of it.\n\nThe mock returns a file NAME, matching the shape the real `/auth/me` sends —\nnot a URL and not an empty string, either of which would let a consumer that\nmishandles one of them pass against this stub.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019zMeXt2VAqLqnTwparBntq\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJql3b2CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA3D4QADITYlvsL1G1dC0LrsRYckTc\ntmRtfBdivohaGC14JVn7n7MbMNkds6aMQNEhbQyVoEIhrGGNSdrXIHSBVjy2whhb\nDeBOnafaY/rALNNNBf9OJ3ipwHaWW+mMglB8Ps47vfZdVuAa4qtgnlBJqy+CeDta\nrt6WuxrEAPWzra+zvC9fPyh1abO85mvUVDeTCkzpTF++JceHWdLTtSPe0IkqheJM\nZyydy02gEFL6sF9ABeSad/++R9kYfIssN6Mo86ZECN4qMsen6o5/dcXRgF1z7Sfl\n2HROqVhfAnYKIObWawguSK/KvTunrGxxBuC16Py+PpMZZtOpqn/QytrDuPBuaBGg\n95mO18EUipQdWZifRm57RSgc4r1How4wVBfCKgA6KepiB42bgkLloQPR8MNJSKVT\nV6FgZFAoGZw7Qnut6Y5/5HbHLuxvsp2fSqPodNdNPtaCd6K+e9Skj6jU8A+4eCKj\nTSCTh80t35VC0+KSIGVJdOJ/5l3UNF3xuCMhNTfZIiTDY6VKyBcT594j+Wj2g1SP\ng64o/2PUZl6Vc58LDlXMikkRCnRkZW6X4GsndUnSe0fDiN2RbY9KBpCAehDAbSt+\nwtIhPGdEoR9757rnxy9ZeHYsP676xLJg+m9lDJDyChJw4pjaJ+qKLnzsHcCtarbZ\najJ0rt/YAHX9RJSobNMm\n=J0Hm\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-02T01:08:06Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"st