lengkapan","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/dfa962a89d8f2c4702101966cc09ad526bd57462/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T02:30:51Z","email":"analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com","name":"analisaperlengkapan"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T02:30:51Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"ci: the SQL guard forked 92 000 processes to read 23 000 lines (#888)\n\n`check-sql-migrations.sh` documents \"Runtime: <1s\". Measured, it takes\n59.6 seconds — and 42.9 of those are `sys`, which is the tell: the work\nis not the scanning, it is the forking.\n\nThe scan loop ran `grep -qiE \"$pat\" <<<\"$code\"` once per line per\npattern. Four patterns over 23 015 lines of migration SQL is roughly\n92 000 processes to answer 92 000 questions that bash can answer itself.\nUsing bash''s own `=~` with `nocasematch` takes it to 3.0 seconds, with\n`sys` falling from 42.9s to 0.12s. Output on the tree as it stands is\nbyte-identical.\n\nByte-identical output on a clean tree only proves the guard invents\nnothing, which is the cheap half. So `--self-test` plants each of the\nfour anti-patterns and asserts all eight instances are flagged, plants\nthe shapes that must NOT flag — a commented-out CONCURRENTLY, a line\nwaived with `-- guard:allow`, PL/pgSQL `DO $$ BEGIN`, `REFRESH ...\nCONCURRENTLY`, and identifiers spelled like keywords (`beginner`,\n`commit_id`) — and asserts the guard stays quiet on all of them, then\nchecks both exit codes. It runs in CI beside the guard.\n\nWhile wiring that up: the guard''s own job could not see the guard.\n`lint-sql` gates on `sql || workflows`, but the script lives at\ninfra/lint/check-sql-migrations.sh, which the detect filters classify as\n`shell`. A PR editing only the guard ran every job except the one that\nruns the guard. `lint-orphan-tests` had the identical hole — its comment\nsays \"workflow changes exercise the guard itself\", but its guard is a\n.sh too. Both gates now include `shell`.\n\nThis is the same shape as #887: the gate was right and its scope was a\nhand-written list that did not grow with what it guards.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WQ99i4hsHtgtPr4pHRk6Vn\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"e3ae251d86c73fe22d14e67a74584599965e64c9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/trees/e3ae251d86c73fe22d14e67a74584599965e64c9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/commits/dfa962a89d8f2c4702101966cc09ad526bd57462","verification":{"payload":"tree e3ae251d86c73fe22d14e67a74584599965e64c9\nparent 0d95fd2dacca638b73b9e0e44ee7990c5fdc8c96\nauthor analisaperlengkapan <analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com> 1788143451 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788143451 +0700\n\nci: the SQL guard forked 92 000 processes to read 23 000 lines (#888)\n\n`check-sql-migrations.sh` documents \"Runtime: <1s\". Measured, it takes\n59.6 seconds — and 42.9 of those are `sys`, which is the tell: the work\nis not the scanning, it is the forking.\n\nThe scan loop ran `grep -qiE \"$pat\" <<<\"$code\"` once per line per\npattern. Four patterns over 23 015 lines of migration SQL is roughly\n92 000 processes to answer 92 000 questions that bash can answer itself.\nUsing bash''s own `=~` with `nocasematch` takes it to 3.0 seconds, with\n`sys` falling from 42.9s to 0.12s. Output on the tree as it stands is\nbyte-identical.\n\nByte-identical output on a clean tree only proves the guard invents\nnothing, which is the cheap half. So `--self-test` plants each of the\nfour anti-patterns and asserts all eight instances are flagged, plants\nthe shapes that must NOT flag — a commented-out CONCURRENTLY, a line\nwaived with `-- guard:allow`, PL/pgSQL `DO $$ BEGIN`, `REFRESH ...\nCONCURRENTLY`, and identifiers spelled like keywords (`beginner`,\n`commit_id`) — and asserts the guard stays quiet on all of them, then\nchecks both exit codes. It runs in CI beside the guard.\n\nWhile wiring that up: the guard''s own job could not see the guard.\n`lint-sql` gates on `sql || workflows`, but the script lives at\ninfra/lint/check-sql-migrations.sh, which the detect filters classify as\n`shell`. A PR editing only the guard ran every job except the one that\nruns the guard. `lint-orphan-tests` had the identical hole — its comment\nsays \"workflow changes exercise the guard itself\", but its guard is a\n.sh too. Both gates now include `shell`.\n\nThis is the same shape as #887: the gate was right and its scope was a\nhand-written list that did not grow with what it guards.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WQ99i4hsHtgtPr4pHRk6Vn\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlOdbCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA7BoQAKZNf+BqqUJIxi6AAyN2TCX1\nsdWhE5hXMXFJEn3bgS+ynv1DkIjsTcygfb+F/Zyh6vI9eqH3rDBjN0tMirwSpdxi\n0bD45iMJtWfVf/D8BPmufM2o4vqy6iEf0B4ULyTFXskcFbRlA7nUcgs155hPxtD+\nKc9+QgjW7OPUdudJBk2a2FzeTtOs5/A8nm8DIK30XMIhM7C3p5lQHIx5NJB6Hg4Q\ncQKUK7ohzuR7GONBKIBIPcSekibxDAhSC/949L+LTXWcGuGTLtZ7G4vrhA/+4res\nosTSg0LnX7AS2Qu6ssd3XX0c2bTtBn1Xkaw7x08CxJmBIPvNXrmGmR+duDBHdcKg\nf30nksZHBCWTR0ZbaGnP4iWzz9Jd4YIbHHONczZczsNkzRSlmNNxnZKjxaTm8c5X\nxI8r/DrGp13eLk2YaPgsNStB3K1PI1UdAGa08/eUAs57UYsyiQImztk5bxGMtxxS\nfU0NKBu/mX1Y0jiKMnqJGt4doKHGmdS03dsU/LWszsZJaQs2aQ2FYd0lbhvAMJby\na70iUwRz6gCW7jrsuO9GHzEdE2zaQJrcrJ95/YPjAgSEkV8y4LysLmPrwU+rPbp8\nJKPKrNIOnYldmSEpoUsal03+Onk80F3imr26wWrbgnKfbut3ztW7lDArBVx87MZM\ndiOlK1sh7x7zIiUg1irs\n=Ut0C\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T02:30:51Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commit/dfa962a89d8f2c4702101966cc09ad526bd57462","node_id":"C_kwDOP93DxNoAKGRmYTk2MmE4OWQ4ZjJjNDcwMjEwMTk2NmNjMDlhZDUyNmJkNTc0NjI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commit/0d95fd2dacca638b73b9e0e44ee7990c5fdc8c96","sha":"0d95fd2dacca638b73b9e0e44ee7990c5fdc8c96","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/0d95fd2dacca638b73b9e0e44ee7990c5fdc8c96"}],"sha":"dfa962a89d8f2c4702101966cc09ad526bd57462","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/dfa962a89d8f2c4702101966cc09ad526bd57462"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/220909633?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan","id":220909633,"login":"analisaperlengkapan","node_id":"U_kgDODSrQQQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/0d95fd2dacca638b73b9e0e44ee7990c5fdc8c96/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T02:30:47Z","email":"analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com","name":"analisaperlengkapan"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T02:30:47Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(perlengkapan): the fixture gave a filing cabinet''s code to a Toyota (#886)\n\nSIMAN''s codification is a function. Over 624 533 assets, 2 033 barang codes map\nto 2 033 names — not one code carries two. (The reverse is not a function: seven\nnames, \"Genset\" and \"Helmet\" among them, are shared by two codes each, which is\nwhy a picker has to return a code and never a name.)\n\nThe e2e seed broke that function, and every code it used was real, so nothing\nlooked wrong:\n\n  3050104001 is \"Lemari Besi/Metal\" (7 428 assets) — the seed filed a Toyota\n             Avanza under it and called it \"Kendaraan Dinas Roda 4\"\n  3050201002 is \"Meja Kerja Kayu\" (43 791) — the seed filed a Honda Vario\n  3100102003 is \"Note Book\" (2 141) — the seed called it \"Personal Computer Unit\"\n  2010101001 is \"Tanah Bangunan Rumah Negara Golongan I\" (166)\n  3100105010 carries no real asset at all\n\nOne fixture row against 43 791 real ones is still a counter-example. It made the\ncodification look many-to-many — the exact property a codification picker has to\nbe able to rely on — and roughly ten e2e assertions asserted the invented\nstrings, so the suite was certifying the fiction rather than catching it.\n\nThe five rows now use the real code for the item each one means, with the name\nSIMAN gives it. Two assertions get sharper as a result: the invented names\nincluded a duplicate, which forced the sort test to check only the two\nunambiguous positions; the real names differ at the first letter, so the whole\nfive-row order is now asserted.\n\nThe guard pins those five codes to the query that produced them, with row\ncounts. A fixture code that is not pinned is a hard failure — which is the\npoint: adding an asset now forces the author to look the name up instead of\ninventing a plausible one. It also checks that every permit''s kode barang still\nmatches the asset its NUP names, since that agreement between two seed files was\nmaintained by hand and I had just changed both. Seven canaries, including the\ntwo \"must still pass\" directions (the committed seeds, and the dotted spelling\nof a code).\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WQ99i4hsHtgtPr4pHRk6Vn\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"092c0c01f517682c4a92c1f89da20709df000360","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/trees/092c0c01f517682c4a92c1f89da20709df000360"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/commits/0d95fd2dacca638b73b9e0e44ee7990c5fdc8c96","verification":{"payload":"tree 092c0c01f517682c4a92c1f89da20709df000360\nparent ae0b53741da32a5bb3a4b3ef500263b8b9f2bbbf\nauthor analisaperlengkapan <analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com> 1788143447 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788143447 +0700\n\nfix(perlengkapan): the fixture gave a filing cabinet''s code to a Toyota (#886)\n\nSIMAN''s codification is a function. Over 624 533 assets, 2 033 barang codes map\nto 2 033 names — not one code carries two. (The reverse is not a function: seven\nnames, \"Genset\" and \"Helmet\" among them, are shared by two codes each, which is\nwhy a picker has to return a code and never a name.)\n\nThe e2e seed broke that function, and every code it used was real, so nothing\nlooked wrong:\n\n  3050104001 is \"Lemari Besi/Metal\" (7 428 assets) — the seed filed a Toyota\n             Avanza under it and called it \"Kendaraan Dinas Roda 4\"\n  3050201002 is \"Meja Kerja Kayu\" (43 791) — the seed filed a Honda Vario\n  3100102003 is \"Note Book\" (2 141) — the seed called it \"Personal Computer Unit\"\n  2010101001 is \"Tanah Bangunan Rumah Negara Golongan I\" (166)\n  3100105010 carries no real asset at all\n\nOne fixture row against 43 791 real ones is still a counter-example. It made the\ncodification look many-to-many — the exact property a codification picker has to\nbe able to rely on — and roughly ten e2e assertions asserted the invented\nstrings, so the suite was certifying the fiction rather than catching it.\n\nThe five rows now use the real code for the item each one means, with the name\nSIMAN gives it. Two assertions get sharper as a result: the invented names\nincluded a duplicate, which forced the sort test to check only the two\nunambiguous positions; the real names differ at the first letter, so the whole\nfive-row order is now asserted.\n\nThe guard pins those five codes to the query that produced them, with row\ncounts. A fixture code that is not pinned is a hard failure — which is the\npoint: adding an asset now forces the author to look the name up instead of\ninventing a plausible one. It also checks that every permit''s kode barang still\nmatches the asset its NUP names, since that agreement between two seed files was\nmaintained by hand and I had just changed both. Seven canaries, including the\ntwo \"must still pass\" directions (the committed seeds, and the dotted spelling\nof a code).\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WQ99i4hsHtgtPr4pHRk6Vn\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlOdXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAzfUQAGRTBYnwKvLmrrbxxyrvEpa1\nj2cO9JqashABmIqw0zsDJooYmUyWlSRV/0Ilr8WKcqHEWXxH2K