heck by eye, including the\nfour nothing looked at — and seeds the values SIMAN really sends: an\nunparseable date, a non-numeric `rph_aset`, a satker with no name.\n\nThe dead-column guard failed this, correctly given what it could see: it\nreads `AS kondisi` as a read of the dead column. A CTE that binds the\ncorrected expression to that name has created a local column, so the\nguard now treats uses AFTER a binding as references to it — positionally,\nbecause `SELECT kondisi AS kondisi` is still a real read and a\nwhole-literal rule would unflag exactly that. Three canaries pin both\ndirections.\n\nThe remaining ~0.8 s is still that regex. It belongs in a generated\nnumeric column, which is a migration rather than a query change.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ti9AujKmDnVjc75wyWsmE\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlFwvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA7mMQAExgsfhHHh6/p8H6wh9/wljN\n07ieyz6VFnxDZpPwyxRNPvl6BJuNe1DoqVlcZx/vddsSQ0mPGbAJEmLOAC+qdRg/\nAJ3lfwUDL4/rWbJMgmHn2LVhN0CpIKoC3tl8gv8oJNKmQviCr5aWyUFzngWRGFSn\nKrKPH6CG1l/BKnUXQB4XGJjysM1oZHR+LrxaYfjsEZmvGcci+NdrgJhYeIUBwTJI\nWjZaZ/V3SWwHawf95qsheuVrRWFUaljTkxCzG+oXHpjRio5zR3ufZhq3wm1BX1Yd\nCLLSxP/RAp1gBuXBuTE1HeTps/XioxLjReA9K0T3RQ2KrF+kOS/S2VdxIevJb3gB\ngzDOHc275K05ZS5+5wvEcsqqWjrzBXla+ie5MzZ0K3aMWEXYWckfDvSkPHBKL4CS\nPzudY2brejzngnPx2tFpsHzqDTSgdmH/YMk+mI9x6fIULMtbbwwV4Rr6mVSdUn2L\nJ6meHpWYVgJYU7BGT4R+EBqG+sZxCeUFXD/3t0gzQUrAKNQBZrFSgedHdCesPAqO\nQ5z4M+s8jItjW+JrCu9JvxQqo9MmqEbHecAL2lefeuNzPj9Q4GfY+R/a44AePDL/\nnXLqOXvgDefa7+XI3FiFc8FOazmNj/bVH7GXhnhFyNADFLbbFH7nV137Zn3GRcMV\n2bKezvAUXsWi8MQDj8lO\n=3zF/\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T16:37:03Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commit/e817cc7d5cc10b359d67b489390aff54f1f9abff","node_id":"C_kwDOP93DxNoAKGU4MTdjYzdkNWNjMTBiMzU5ZDY3YjQ4OTM5MGFmZjU0ZjFmOWFiZmY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commit/75294cd383fab336c39ed303d8a0e1ac72fc63e8","sha":"75294cd383fab336c39ed303d8a0e1ac72fc63e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/75294cd383fab336c39ed303d8a0e1ac72fc63e8"}],"sha":"e817cc7d5cc10b359d67b489390aff54f1f9abff","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/e817cc7d5cc10b359d67b489390aff54f1f9abff"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/220909633?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/analisaperlengkapan","id":220909633,"login":"analisaperlengkapan","node_id":"U_kgDODSrQQQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/analisaperlengkapan","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/commits/75294cd383fab336c39ed303d8a0e1ac72fc63e8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:10:38Z","email":"analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com","name":"analisaperlengkapan"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:10:38Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(perlengkapan): the employee lookup never found anybody (#882)\n\n* fix(perlengkapan): the employee lookup never found anybody\n\n`integrasi.mysimkari_pegawai.satker_id` is TEXT, and every satker-scoped\nemployee query read it as the MySIMKARI `kode_satker`. It holds\n`mysimkari_satker.api_id` — the upstream API''s UUID. Measured on staging over\n21 328 rows: 21 325 join to `api_id`, exactly 3 match a `kode_satker`. For one\nsatker, `WHERE satker_id = ''01.01''` returned 0 while the resolved join returns\n123. 191 satkers have people who were unreachable.\n\nThe consequences were not cosmetic. The pakaian-dinas roster was empty for\nevery satker. The profile upsert splices the caller''s scope as an extra AND on\nthe SoT row, so for an operator_satker it selected nothing, inserted nothing,\nand returned success — the flow worked only for cross-satker roles, whose\nscope adds no predicate. `kode_satker` was written from `p.satker_id`, a UUID\ninto a column named for a code. And `cek-pegawai/{nip}`, which exists\nprecisely so an operator types a NIP instead of retyping five fields, could\nonly ever answer \"Pegawai tidak ditemukan\" — which is why the request forms\nare filled in by hand.\n\nThree things hid it. The integration fixture seeded a kode_satker into that\ncolumn, a shape the real table never has, so the tests ran against a table\nwhere the broken query worked. The roster tests asserted only 200, and an\nendpoint answering 200 with an empty list looks exactly like one that works.\nAnd the doc comment above the query asserted the wrong column outright.\n\nshared/pegawai_ref.rs is now the one place that knows how an employee is tied\nto a satker, with the measurement written down beside it. The join is LEFT on\npurpose: 3 rows resolve to no satker, and a cross-satker reader should see\nthem rather than have them disappear.\n\ncek-pegawai now derives its scope from the claims instead of `?satker_id=`,\nwhich had let any authenticated caller read another satker''s employee record\nby naming it. The scope is a predicate inside the query, so an out-of-scope\nemployee is simply not there — no second check to forget, and no answer that\ndistinguishes \"exists elsewhere\" from \"does not exist\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Ybw1TonfE4XqdYdqpLP3Tc\n\n* fix(integrasi): the same wrong column, in the gRPC employee lookup\n\nThe unit tests that failed CI were asserting the column this branch\nreplaces: `p.satker_id` holds the upstream API''s `api_id`, not a\n`kode_satker`, so a scope predicate on it matched nothing. They are\nrewritten to derive their expectation from `PEGAWAI_KODE_SATKER_SQL`\nand to carry the negative — a hand-typed literal is what let them keep\npassing while the column underneath them changed.\n\nChasing why the e2e went red found the third instance, and this one is\nin the data plane. `GetMysimkariPegawai` filtered `WHERE satker_id = $1`\nwith a `kode_satker`, and projected `COALESCE(satker_id,'''') AS\nkode_satker` — so it returned zero rows for every satker-filtered call\nand handed a UUID to every consumer under the name of a code. Measured\non staging: filtering by code reaches 3 satkers, joining through\n`api_id` reaches 191. Kejaksaan Agung alone has 5 506 employees this RPC\ncould never return. It is the second always-empty defect in this one\nRPC; the comment above the columns records the first, and both hid for\nthe same reason — an empty list is a valid response, so nothing errored.\n\nBoth e2e seeds wrote the code into that column and a comment asserted\nthat was its meaning, so every test standing on them certified the bug.\nThey now derive the value by joining the satkers they already seed, and\nupsert it, because an environment seeded before this fix would otherwise\nkeep the wrong value forever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ti9AujKmDnVjc75wyWsmE\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"5fae1a6cce11ec40442b143b82cf73639e26f0a5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/trees/5fae1a6cce11ec40442b143b82cf73639e26f0a5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/analisaperlengkapan/simpel2/git/commits/75294cd383fab336c39ed303d8a0e1ac72fc63e8","verification":{"payload":"tree 5fae1a6cce11ec40442b143b82cf73639e26f0a5\nparent ea0e212f49f903956c88614656a801c8fd05c42d\nauthor analisaperlengkapan <analisaperlengkapan@gmail.com> 1788055838 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788055838 +0000\n\nfix(perlengkapan): the employee lookup never found anybody (#882)\n\n* fix(perlengkapan): the employee lookup never found anybody\n\n`integrasi.mysimkari_pegawai.satker_id` is TEXT, and every satker-scoped\nemployee query read it as the MySIMKARI `kode_satker`. It holds\n`mysimkari_satker.api_id` — the upstream API''s UUID. Measured on staging over\n21 328 rows: 21 325 join to `api_id`, exactly 3 match a `kode_satker`. For one\nsatker, `WHERE satker_id = ''01.01''` returned 0 while the resolved join returns\n123. 191 satkers have people who were unreachable.\n\nThe consequences were not cosmetic. The pakaian-dinas roster was empty for\nevery satker. The profile upsert splices the caller''s scope as an extra AND on\nthe SoT row, so for an operator_satker it selected nothing, inserted nothing,\nand returned success — the flow worked only for cross-satker roles, whose\nscope adds no predicate. `kode_satker` was written from `p.satker_id`, a UUID\ninto a column named for a code. And `cek-pegawai/{nip}`, which exists\nprecisely so an operator types a NIP instead of retyping five fields, could\nonly ever answer \"Pegawai tidak ditemukan\" — which is why the request forms\nare filled in by hand.\n\nThree things hid it. The integration fixture seeded a kode_satker into that\ncolumn, a shape the real table never has, so the tests ran against a table\nwhere the broken query worked. The roster tests asserted only 200, and an\nendpoint answering 200 with an empty list looks exactly like one that works.\nAnd the doc comment above the query asserted the wrong column outright.\n\nshared/pegawai_ref.rs is now the one place that knows how an employee is tied\nto a satker, with the measurement written down beside it. The join is LEFT on\npurpose: 3 rows resolve to no satker, and a cross-satker reader should see\nthem rather than have them disappear.\n\ncek-pegawai now derives its scope from the claims instead of `?satker_id=`,\nwhich had let any authenticated caller read another satker''s employee record\nby naming it. The scope is a predicate inside the query, so an out-of-scope\nemployee is simply not there — no second check to forget, and no answer that\ndistinguishes \"exists elsewhere\" from \"does not exist\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Ybw1TonfE4XqdYdqpLP3Tc\n\n* fix(integrasi): the same wrong column, in the gRPC employee lookup\n\nThe unit tests that failed CI were asserting the column this branch\nreplaces: `p.satker_id` holds the upstream API''s `api_id`, not a\n`kode_satker`, so a scope predicate on it matched nothing. They are\nrewritten to derive their expectation from `PEGAWAI_KODE_SATKER_SQL`\nand to carry the negative — a hand-typed literal is what let them keep\npassing while the column underneath them changed.\n\nChasing why the e2e went red found the third instance, and this one is\nin the data plane. `GetMysimkariPegawai` filtered `WHERE satker_id = $1`\nwith a `kode_satker`, and projected `COALESCE(satker_id,'''') AS\nkode_satker` — so it returned zero rows for every satker-filtered call\nand handed a UUID to every consumer under the name of a code. Measured\non staging: filtering by code reaches 3 satkers, joining through\n`api_id` reaches 191. Kejaksaan Agung alone has 5 506 employees this RPC\ncould never return. It is the second always-empty defect in this one\nRPC; the comment above the columns records the first, and both hid for\nthe same reason — an empty list is a valid response, so nothing errored.\n\nBoth e2e seeds wrote the code into that column and a comment asserted\nthat was its meaning, so every test standing on them certified the bug.\nThey now derive the value by joining the satkers they already seed, and\nupsert it, because an environment seeded before this fix would otherwise\nkeep the wrong value forever.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ti9AujKmDnVjc75wyWsmE\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk5EeCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAayYQAFFWq0ZWPSGyTOXMSyK/GqKe\nEm9mA5tTguF1rMQ+TGQ0YE4rga8RQPLrcqY3zBDCZx9kfsk8zyOwM06kI2imqr+H\nSWApt86eGl2yCglnLz+u6G2lGvCZB4GX2LV6sTxYxjX3j7gq3c5PBy5mXGrArj5E\nlYDBfVT/feFo2teYmt4is6HhygIKAyvDOWZQPpwuRqbJoEaq+nJRN3G6wfKzGPVS\nfOxguigeLJVD4JwZJUbyroI/sUa3SlnPABqjVkaDfdSubBo5znxXO59GPtUhZ48P\nnjIOji9TWJCvdu0oW4ny2lnaviO1/K2Arz3mCh7xhhfMpyIcFs5pOAkuaq8XJWqK\nfRwI7bWcCUHix1bRqRC6XS6IdRl