pe":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/v15a1","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opensuperapp/opensuperapp/commits/1212f7bb5567c25ba43980e8dfef37dc66e08f64/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-26T15:08:05Z","email":"visalrajapakse@gmail.com","name":"v15a1"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-26T15:20:03Z","email":"visalrajapakse@gmail.com","name":"v15a1"},"message":"[Backend] Feature: Surface business-card claims from the access token\n\nThe business card is derived entirely from the caller''s access token. Every\nfield it renders already arrives as a claim on the Asgardeo access token —\n`given_name`, `family_name`, `jobtitle`, `phone_number` and `profile` — and\nlands in `JwtPayload` through its `json...` rest field. They are simply never\nextracted into the context record, so callers cannot see them.\n\n- Declare the five claims as typed optional fields on `JwtPayload`, using the\n  Asgardeo claim names verbatim. The rest field stays, so nothing else changes.\n- Add nilable camelCase counterparts to `CustomJwtPayload` and map them across\n  in `JwtInterceptor`, mirroring the existing `userid` -> `userId` rename.\n\n`CustomJwtPayload` is closed, so the interceptor literal is forced to supply\nevery field — intentional, it keeps the mapping honest.\n\nThis replaces the HR-GraphQL and SCIM lookup approach: with the claims in the\ncontext there is no user id -> email resolution and no HR round-trip left to\nmake, so no `entity` or `scim` additions are needed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"cebaaf9ed84f0e72d22686773c10547846edb7da","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opensuperapp/opensuperapp/git/trees/cebaaf9ed84f0e72d22686773c10547846edb7da"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opensuperapp/opensuperapp/git/commits/1212f7bb5567c25ba43980e8dfef37dc66e08f64","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verifi